  "body": "The MCP server (#128) can add a Drill, Bore or Thread operation, but it can't fill the operation's hole list. Those kinds machine only the holes stored in their parameters, which the app fills from its **Detect holes** button. So over MCP a drill on a model with holes always refuses.\n\n**Reproduced on `main` 8f30bee** (`camcraft-cli mcp`): `import_model` `testdata/models/Milling-test.step`, then `set_stock` `from_model`, `add_tool` `DR-0600`, `add_operation` `{kind: \"drill\", source: \"part\"}` and `generate`. The result is \"Op: nothing to machine: no holes to drill\". Bore with `EM-0300-2FL` gives \"boring needs at least one hole\".\n\n**Root cause (confirmed):** the hole list is filled only by the UI's `State::detect_holes` (`crates/ui/src/state.rs`). It reads `operation::holes_of` (and `cross_holes_of` on a turret machine) and commits `EditOperationParams`. The MCP tool table (`crates/app/src/mcp/tools.rs`) has no tool that does this, and `edit_operation` would need the agent to compute hole coordinates itself.\n\n**Fix:** move the rule out of the UI into `operation` (one function: the op's source, recognised, written into whichever hole list the kind has, plus the radial list on a turret machine, refusing by name otherwise). The UI's button and a new MCP tool `detect_holes {name}` both call it, so an agent's holes and a click's are the same, and the edit is one undo step. Pin: an MCP test detects the holes of a plate with a through hole, generates the drill, and `undo` empties the list again.\n\nRelated: #180 (MCP picks for faces, edges and loops) is the same gap for area operations.\n\nSuggested labels (I can't set them): `enhancement`, `P2`, `area:ui`, `source:agent`.",
